%I A397074 #10 Oct 22 2026 20:14:28
%S A397074 1,2,7,125,355251
%N A397074 Number of NPN-equivalence classes of Boolean functions of n variables whose sensitivity is equal to n.
%C A397074 The sensitivity s(f) of a Boolean function f is the maximum over all inputs x of the number of coordinates i such that flipping the i-th bit of x changes the value of f. Here we count the NPN-equivalence classes (functions up to negation of input variables, permutation of input variables, and negation of the output) of n-variable Boolean functions attaining the maximum possible sensitivity, s(f) = n.
%C A397074 From _Alex Towell_, Oct 17 2026: (Start)
%C A397074 For every Boolean function f on n variables, the conditions s(f)=n, bs(f)=n and C(f)=n are equivalent. Thus, for every n, this sequence also counts NPN classes with full block sensitivity or full certificate complexity.
%C A397074 To see this, fix an input x. If there are n disjoint nonempty sensitive blocks, each must be a single coordinate, so bs(f,x)=n exactly when s(f,x)=n. For certificate complexity, if changing one bit leaves the output unchanged, fixing the other n-1 coordinates gives a certificate at x. If every single-bit neighbor has the opposite output, every coordinate must be fixed in a certificate. Thus C(f,x)=n exactly when s(f,x)=n as well. Taking maxima over x proves the equivalence. (End)
%C A397074 a(5) = 355251 was obtained from a complete census of all 616126 NPN classes of Boolean functions on at most 5 variables.
%Y A397074 Cf. A000370 (number of NPN-equivalence classes of Boolean functions of n or fewer variables), A397075 (degree), A397076 (deterministic decision-tree complexity).
%K A397074 nonn,hard,more,changed
%O A397074 1,2
%A A397074 _Alex Towell_, Jun 15 2026
